  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"The conductor has chosen the musical cues; rehearsal can connect those cues to score markings and visible conducting gestures.\", \"decision\": \"How should singers learn shared entrances and cutoffs?\", \"goal\": \"Make rehearsals easy to attend and prepare for while helping singers use shared rehearsal time effectively.\", \"problem\": \"A community choir is preparing a small program through a series of rehearsals. It has a conductor, willing section leaders, a booking organizer, numbered scores that members may use, and shared practice recordings. The rehearsal room has movable chairs and enough space for short sectional work. Members have varied experience and availability. Musical direction remains with the conductor, and each routine below can operate within any agreed rehearsal schedule.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nThe conductor demonstrates each entrance and cutoff while singers mark the matching cue in their scores before singing. A written reminder is established before the choir tries to follow the same gesture in real time.\nSection leaders first show the chosen score cues to their singers, and the conductor then rehearses the corresponding gestures with everyone. Local preparation connects the written instructions to a single full-choir demonstration of the agreed timing.\nThe choir tries the chosen entrances and cutoffs while watching the conductor, then marks its scores after discussing uncertainty. The markings respond to cues that proved difficult during an actual attempt at the shared musical action."
    }
  ],
